---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
import SafetyExplorer from '../components/SafetyExplorer.tsx';
import {
  ADVISORY_LEVEL_COUNTS,
  COUNTRIES,
  FIT,
  GLOBAL_VICTIMIZATION,
  META,
  byName,
} from '../lib/safety';
import ogImage from '../assets/og-safety.png';

const eg = byName('Egypt')!;
const us = byName('United States')!;
const withHomicide = COUNTRIES.filter((c) => c.homicide !== null).length;
const withAdvisory = COUNTRIES.filter((c) => c.advisory !== null).length;

// Every falsifiable claim on this page points at one of these.
const S = {
  gallup2025:
    'https://www.gallup.com/file/analytics/695138/Gallup_Global-Safety-Report-2025.pdf',
  gallupSlip: 'https://news.gallup.com/poll/650516/global-safety-starts-slip.aspx',
  lawOrder: 'https://www.gallup.com/analytics/694904/what-is-law-order-index.aspx',
  sdg: 'https://unstats.un.org/sdgs/report/2025/goal-16/',
  owid: 'https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/homicide-rate-unodc',
  unodc:
    'https://www.unodc.org/unodc/en/data-and-analysis/global-study-on-homicide.html',
  advisories: 'https://travel.state.gov/_res/rss/TAsTWs.xml',
  deaths: 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12782918/',
  unwomen:
    'https://www.gisf.ngo/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/2172-UN-Women-2013-Study-on-Ways-and-Methods-to-Eliminate-Sexual-Harassment-in-Egypt.pdf',
  gpi: 'https://www.visionofhumanity.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Global-Peace-Index-2025-web.pdf',
  gti:
    'https://www.visionofhumanity.org/global-terrorism-falls-to-a-decade-low-but-western-fatalities-surge/',
  eisner:
    'https://ourworldindata.org/data-insights/homicide-rates-have-declined-dramatically-over-the-centuries',
  source: 'https://github.com/MaxGhenis/maxghenis.com/blob/master/scripts/generate-safety-data.py',
};
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <BaseHead
      title={`Felt safety vs. recorded safety | ${SITE_TITLE}`}
      description="An interactive comparison of how safe people say they feel with what crime statistics and travel advisories actually record, across 144 countries. Perceived safety explains 54% of the variance in homicide rates — the interesting part is the other 46%."
      image={ogImage}
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <Header />
    <main>
      <header class="page-header">
        <h1>Felt safety vs. recorded safety</h1>
        <p class="page-description">
          Two independent measurements of the same thing — what people say
          about walking home at night, and what the coroner's office counts.
          They agree about half the time. Where they part company is the
          interesting part.
        </p>
      </header>

      <SafetyExplorer client:load />

      <div class="intro prose">
        <p class="callout">
          Across the {FIT.n} countries carrying both measurements, the recorded
          homicide rate explains <strong>{Math.round(FIT.r2 * 100)}% of the
          variance</strong> in how safe people say they feel (r = {FIT.r.toFixed(2)}).
          That is a strong result for a one-question survey measured against an
          entirely separate administrative source. It also leaves {100 - Math.round(FIT.r2 * 100)}%
          unexplained, and that residual is where the argument lives.
        </p>

        <h2>Why perception is on the axis at all</h2>
        <p>
          Homicide is close to the only crime statistic that compares across
          borders. Assault, theft, and rape rates are dominated by whether
          people report to police, and reporting rises with police trust and
          state capacity — so a country that improves its policing records
          <em>more</em> crime, not less. Comparing raw assault rates between two
          countries mostly measures their institutions.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is why the UN put a survey question, not a police statistic,
          in <a href={S.sdg} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sustainable
          Development Goal indicator 16.1.4</a>: "Do you feel safe walking
          alone at night in the city or area where you live?"
          <a href={S.gallup2025} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gallup</a> is
          the only organization that asks it worldwide every year —
          {META.gallupRespondents.toLocaleString()} adults across
          {META.gallupCountries} countries in the {META.fieldYear} field year.
        </p>
        <p>
          Gallup's <a href={S.lawOrder} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Law and
          Order Index</a> is only half perception. Two of its four components
          ask what happened to you, not how you feel: whether you had money or
          property stolen in the past 12 months ({GLOBAL_VICTIMIZATION.theftPct}%
          globally), and whether you were assaulted or mugged
          ({GLOBAL_VICTIMIZATION.assaultPct}%).
        </p>

        <h2>Reading the gap</h2>
        <p>
          The dashed line fits felt safety to the log of the recorded homicide
          rate. A country above it feels safer than its body count predicts; a
          country below it feels less safe. Egypt sits
          <strong>{eg.residual! > 0 ? '+' : ''}{eg.residual}</strong> points
          above the line — the same direction as, and smaller than, the
          United States at
          <strong>{us.residual! > 0 ? '+' : ''}{us.residual}</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          A positive gap is not automatically distortion. Where violence is
          geographically concentrated, the national mean overstates what the
          median resident actually faces, and the gap opens without anyone
          shading their answer. Heavy visible policing can open it too, and so
          can reluctance to tell a pollster your neighborhood is dangerous.
          <strong>The residual cannot tell those apart.</strong> It marks where
          to look, not what you'll find.
        </p>

        <h2>What this does not measure</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Harassment.</strong> It is in none of these series — not
            homicide, not "assaulted or mugged," not really the walking-alone
            question as most people parse it. A
            <a href={S.unwomen} target="_blank" rel="noopener">2013 UN Women
            study</a> found 99.3% of Egyptian women had experienced sexual
            harassment, 89% of incidents on the street. That coexists with
            Egypt's {eg.feltSafe}% headline without contradiction, because they
            are different constructs.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Visitors.</strong> Every perception figure samples
            residents about their own neighborhood. A resident's commute and a
            foreigner's afternoon in a tourist district are different draws
            from different distributions.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Recency, unevenly.</strong> UNODC homicide readings arrive
            on each country's own schedule. Only {withHomicide} of
            {COUNTRIES.length} countries here have a reading from 2015 or
            later; Egypt's is {eg.homicideYear}. Every figure carries its year.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Rare, severe events.</strong> A homicide rate is an average
            over a population. It says nothing about low-frequency,
            high-severity risk aimed specifically at foreigners, which is a
            different statistical shape and the one that historically drove
            tourist risk in several countries on this page.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The traveler layer</h2>
        <p>
          The "For travelers" view carries current
          <a href={S.advisories} target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. State
          Department advisories</a> — pulled from the official feed, with the
          date each was last issued. The feed covers
          {Object.values(ADVISORY_LEVEL_COUNTS).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0)}
          destinations across all four levels
          ({ADVISORY_LEVEL_COUNTS['1']} at level 1, {ADVISORY_LEVEL_COUNTS['2']}
          at level 2, {ADVISORY_LEVEL_COUNTS['3']} at level 3, and
          {ADVISORY_LEVEL_COUNTS['4']} at level 4); {withAdvisory} of them match
          a country Gallup polls, and those are the ones the table shows.
        </p>
        <p>
          Against that, what actually kills travelers: across 15,549
          non-natural deaths of U.S. citizens abroad from 2002 to 2022,
          <a href={S.deaths} target="_blank" rel="noopener">vehicle accidents
          account for 29% and homicide 21%</a>, with war and terrorism inside a
          6% remainder. That study reports proportions rather than rates,
          because — as its authors note — reliable counts of how many Americans
          are in each country simply do not exist. Deaths per visitor by
          country is not computable from public data, so this page does not
          compute it.
        </p>

        <h2>How it's built</h2>
        <p>
          <a href={S.source} target="_blank" rel="noopener">One
          script</a> joins four public sources on country name and writes a
          single JSON file; the page reads only that file. The fit is ordinary
          least squares on {FIT.form}. Country-name reconciliation across four
          naming conventions is the fiddly part, and the generator prints every
          country that fails to join rather than dropping it quietly — 30
          countries have no recent UNODC reading, and the join report names
          them. Tests pin the headline numbers, so regenerating the data with
          different results fails the build instead of silently updating the
          prose.
        </p>

        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ul class="sources">
          <li>
            <a href={S.gallup2025} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gallup, The
            Global Safety Report 2025</a> — walking-alone percentages and Law
            and Order Index, {META.fieldYear} field year
            (<a href={S.gallupSlip} target="_blank" rel="noopener">summary</a>).
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={S.owid} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Our World in Data</a>,
            from the <a href={S.unodc} target="_blank" rel="noopener">UNODC
            Global Study on Homicide</a> — homicide rates and time series.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={S.advisories} target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. State
            Department travel advisories</a> (official RSS).
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={S.deaths} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Analysis of the
            non-natural deaths of US citizens while abroad</a>, 2002–2022.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={S.gpi} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Global Peace Index
            2025</a> and <a href={S.gti} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Global
            Terrorism Index</a> — context for conflict trends, which move
            separately from street safety.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={S.eisner} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Our World in Data
            on the long-run homicide decline</a>.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </main>
    <Footer />

    <style>
      main {
        max-width: 1040px;
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 2rem 1.5rem 4rem;
      }
      .page-header {
        margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
      }
      .page-description {
        color: var(--text-muted);
        font-size: var(--text-lg);
        max-width: 640px;
      }
      .intro {
        margin: 0 0 2rem;
      }
      .intro.prose {
        max-width: 760px;
        margin-left: 0;
      }
      .callout {
        background: var(--rule-glow);
        border-left: 4px solid var(--rule);
        padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
        border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;
        color: var(--text-secondary);
      }
      .sources {
        font-size: var(--text-sm);
      }
      .sources li {
        margin-bottom: 0.5em;
      }
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
